## CSRF

- `middleware/csrf_guard.py` rejects `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE` without `X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest` with 403 `CSRF_HEADER_MISSING`; the Stripe webhook (signature-verified) and health routes are exempt
- CORS allows only `settings.cors_origin` with `allow_credentials=True`, so a foreign origin cannot pass the preflight the header forces; no token endpoint, no double-submit cookie

---

## Rate Limiting

- A Redis-backed fixed-window limiter in `middleware/rate_limit.py`, keyed on `request.client.host`, the IP uvicorn resolved through the trusted-proxy chain (Containers); it never parses `X-Forwarded-For` itself, because every entry but the last is client-supplied and keying on the first hop lets one client rotate buckets
- 100 requests per 15 minutes globally; 10 per 15 minutes on the auth routes, listed by full `/v1` path (`/v1/auth/login`, `/register`, `/forgot-password`, `/reset-password`), since a pattern without the mount prefix never matches and leaves the route on the global limit
- Over the limit: 429 `RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` with `Retry-After`
- Without `REDIS_URL` it falls back to an in-process counter with one `rate_limiter_in_memory` warning, in development and test only; production refuses to start without `REDIS_URL`, because per-process counters let an attacker rotate across instances past the auth limit
- Skipped when `settings.environment == "test"`; rate-limit tests turn it back on

---

## Idempotency Keys

- `middleware/idempotency.py` handles `POST` and `PUT` with an `Idempotency-Key` from an authenticated user. A new key claims a `request_idempotency_keys` row (`key`, `user_id`, `request_method`, `request_path`, `request_body_hash`, `state`, `locked_until`, `claim_token uuid`, `status_code`, `response_body jsonb`; unique on `(key, user_id)`), runs the handler, and stores the result as `completed`
- A `completed` key within 24 hours replays the stored response only when method, path, and SHA-256 body hash all match; any mismatch answers 422 `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED` and runs nothing
- A claim holds a 60-second lease: a request meeting a live `in_progress` claim answers 409, and one meeting an expired lease takes it over with a single `UPDATE ... WHERE state = 'in_progress' AND locked_until < now() RETURNING key`, so two takeovers cannot both win. Completion and the `finally` release after a raise or 5xx both carry `AND claim_token = :mine`, so a slow original cannot overwrite or delete the claim that replaced it
- The hourly cleanup job deletes rows older than 24 hours

---

## Request Timeout

`middleware/request_timeout.py` wraps the downstream call in `asyncio.timeout(30)` and answers 408 `SERVER_REQUEST_TIMEOUT`; streaming routes (SSE) are exempt and enforce their own idle timeout.

