## Error Handling and Response Envelope

Every success is `{ "data": ... }` (plus `"meta"` for pages); every error is `{ "code": "...", "error": "..." }`, the envelope the Express template returns, so one frontend `apiFetch` handles both backends.

- Codes live in `constants/error_codes.py` as a `StrEnum` namespaced `DOMAIN_REASON` (`AUTH_REQUIRED`, `TRIPS_NOT_FOUND`), each with a one-line comment saying when it fires; an unregistered code is a type error, and clients switch on the code, never the message
- `app/errors.py` defines `AppError(status_code, code, message)` and subclasses (`NotFoundError`, `ConflictError`, `ForbiddenError`)
- `register_exception_handlers` installs five handlers: `AppError` to its own status and code; Starlette's `HTTPException` to `ROUTING_NOT_FOUND` or `ROUTING_METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED` (no response uses FastAPI's default `{ detail }`); `RequestValidationError` to 400 `INPUT_VALIDATION_ERROR`; connection-class `OperationalError` and asyncpg connection errors to 503 `SERVER_DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE`; bare `Exception` to 500 `SERVER_INTERNAL_ERROR`
- The 500 handler logs with `exc_info`, reports to Sentry with the request ID, and returns `"Internal server error"` in production (the message elsewhere, never a traceback)
- A unique violation is caught where a useful message exists (`IntegrityError` with `orig.sqlstate == "23505"` on register becomes 409 `AUTH_EMAIL_ALREADY_REGISTERED`), never mapped globally; cache and analytics failures degrade (catch the specific exception, log, continue) (R-344)

